  "name": "Opéra bouffe (Offenbach-era Parisian operetta)",
  "family": "classical",
  "lineage": "Comic operetta genre of Second Empire Paris, created by Hervé and above all by Jacques Offenbach, who opened the Théâtre des Bouffes-Parisiens in 1855. Works such as Orphée aux enfers (1858), La belle Hélène (1864) and La vie parisienne (1866) parodied classical myth and contemporary society through spoken dialogue, strophic couplets, patter songs, ensemble finales and galop dance numbers, and the genre became a model for Viennese and English operetta. The generic strings and flute cards stand in for the theatre orchestra's string section and flutes.",
